rollback: to generation 0000 (`(the state before the first switch)`)

observed:
  owned link     <HOME>/.config/foot
  owned link     <HOME>/.config/hypr
  owned link     <HOME>/.config/btop  (no longer managed after this)

applying 9 operation(s):
  stage link   <HOME>/.config/foot.rp-tmp-0 -> <HOME>/rice/old/foot
  stage link   <HOME>/.config/hypr.rp-tmp-1 -> <HOME>/rice/old/hypr
  exchange     <HOME>/.config/foot <-> <HOME>/.config/foot.rp-tmp-0
  exchange     <HOME>/.config/hypr <-> <HOME>/.config/hypr.rp-tmp-1
  to attic     <HOME>/.config/foot.rp-tmp-0 -> <attic><HOME>/.config/foot
  to attic     <HOME>/.config/hypr.rp-tmp-1 -> <attic><HOME>/.config/hypr
  to attic     <HOME>/.config/btop -> <attic><HOME>/.config/btop
  fsync dir    <HOME>/.config
  fsync dir    <HOME>/.local/state/ricepilot/attic/<TS>-1

applied. this is generation 0002.

displaced links are in:
  <HOME>/.local/state/ricepilot/attic/<TS>-1
nothing was removed.

1 destination(s) are no longer managed. ricepilot cannot make a path absent —
that would be a deletion — so their links were moved into the attic above:
  <HOME>/.config/btop

to undo this:
  ricepilot rollback --commit            re-applies generation 0001
  sh <HOME>/.local/state/ricepilot/rescue.sh   the same thing, from a TTY, without ricepilot

this changed the filesystem, not the running session. log out and back in to see it:
  uwsm stop

never `hyprctl dispatch exit` and never kill Hyprland — `uwsm stop` is the clean
logout. if the session does not come back, TTY F2–F6 and run the rescue script above.
